<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Zimbabwe</h1><p class="st-meta">1220 CE – 1450 CE (approx.) · capital Great Zimbabwe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The builders of Great Zimbabwe, whose dry-stone city controlled the gold trade of the southern African plateau for over two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the decline of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mapungubwe">Kingdom of Mapungubwe</a> in the early 13th century, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a>-speaking elite on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwe plateau</a> took over the routes carrying gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> to the coast. Building at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> began with walls on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-complex-great-zimbabwe">Hill Complex</a> and grew into the largest stone-built settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a> south of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-complex-great-zimbabwe">Hill Complex</a>, while the Great Enclosure, with its conical tower and walls up to 11 metres high, probably served royal or ritual purposes. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, farming and gold mined across the plateau and sent via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa</a>. Finds include soapstone bird carvings, Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, Persian pottery and a Kilwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coin. The city may have held 10,000 to 18,000 people.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> declined from the mid-15th century, perhaps through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/overgrazing">overgrazing</a>, exhausted resources and a northward shift of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a>. Its heirs were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> state in the north and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-butua">Kingdom of Butua</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a> in the south-west. The ruins gave modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> its name and its national emblem, the Zimbabwe bird.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Zimbabwe" width="320" height="407"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cefala_Manich_Simbaoe.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1220 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Great Zimbabwe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Shona religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shona</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, <a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Zimbabwe, Zimbabwe State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1335&amp;focus=kingdom-of-zimbabwe" aria-label="Kingdom of Zimbabwe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5514 2928 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Zimbabwe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5834 3184L5833 3185M5834 3184L5783 3253M5834 3184L5766 3179"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5833 3185h0M5783 3253h0M5766 3179h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5834 3184h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5834 3184h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Great Zimbabwe. <a href="/atlas?year=1335&amp;focus=kingdom-of-zimbabwe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
